= Mental simulation — Simple =

<onlyinclude>'''Mental simulation''' is the human ability to imagine events, actions or possible situations without directly experiencing them at that moment. People may rehearse a conversation, imagine a future journey or ask what might have happened if a past event had gone differently. These simulations can support planning and learning, but their plausibility does not make them accurate predictions.<ref name="schacter">Daniel L. Schacter, Donna Rose Addis and Randy L. Buckner, [https://doi.org/10.1038/nrn2213 “Remembering the past to imagine the future: the prospective brain”], ''Nature Reviews Neuroscience'' 8, 657–661 (2007).</ref></onlyinclude>

== How does it work in everyday life? ==

Suppose you are preparing for a job interview. You might picture several possible questions, practice answers and imagine how you would respond to unexpected changes. That is one form of mental simulation.

Someone may also replay an earlier interview, asking what would have changed if they had answered differently. Those imagined alternatives are called '''counterfactuals'''. Imagining an event that has not happened is prospective simulation.

Researchers study several related phenomena under these labels. They are not all produced by one demonstrated brain mechanism.

== What can mental simulation help us do? ==

* Rehearse a difficult action before performing it.
* Consider several possible outcomes of a decision.
* Prepare for obstacles and unexpected situations.
* Understand another person's possible perspective, without claiming to read their mind.
* Reevaluate a past outcome and extract lessons.

These possible benefits depend on how accurate the imagined scenario is. Repeating an unlikely disaster scenario can also create unnecessary worry or distort a person's risk judgment.

== The simulation heuristic ==

Psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky described a '''simulation heuristic''': people may use the ease of constructing an imagined scenario when making judgments. An event that is easy to imagine can feel more plausible even when its objective probability is not higher.<ref name="kahneman">Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky, [https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/judgment-under-uncertainty/simulation-heuristic/9C6ED91A6429A8443641641DC41A52FE “The simulation heuristic”], in ''Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases'' (1982).</ref>

The heuristic is a research account of judgment, not a rule that every easily imagined event will occur.

== Imagining the future and remembering the past ==

Neuroscience research has examined how imagining future episodes engages brain networks also involved in remembering personal experiences. This overlap can help explain how people combine elements of the past into new possibilities. It does not establish that every imagined future event is a replay of memory or that brain imaging can verify the truth of a mental scenario.<ref name="schacter"/>

== What makes a simulation useful? ==

Useful simulations specify a goal, consider alternatives and compare imagined expectations with what actually happens. Rehearsing only one favorable scenario may lead to overconfidence. For planning, it may be better to include realistic obstacles and verify important assumptions.

A mental image is evidence about what someone imagines, not direct evidence about the state of the external world.

== Simulation in computers is different ==

Computer simulations also explore possible states or future trajectories, but they execute explicit mathematical or software models. That shared word does not prove that an artificial agent has human episodic imagery, autobiographical memory or the same neural mechanisms.

[[Scientific model]] describes computational representations, while [[Cognition]] and [[Reasoning]] describe other relevant research contexts.

